    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.files\r\n\r\n### Description\r\n\r\n`MiniFiles.open` currently errors out when passed a path to a non-existent file:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/5eb6ae150d472dd35d5ecfd933fbef2a187c71dc/lua/mini/files.lua#L669-L674\r\n\r\nBut it's very likely that a user might want to navigate a directory that doesn't exist yet. e.g.:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ ls -d foo\r\nfoo/\r\n$ ls -a foo\r\n./  ../  bar\r\n$ nvim foo/baz\r\n...\r\n```\r\n\r\nOpening mini.files here should show `foo/`. For reference, this usage pattern is supported by [vinegar](https://github.com/tpope/vim-vinegar/).\r\n\r\nI suggest that mini.files should allow, as a special case, a `path` pointing to a non-existent file in a directory that does exist. Something along the lines of:\r\n\r\n```diff\r\n  local fs_type = H.fs_get_type(path)\r\n+  if fs_type == nil then\r\n+    path = H.fs_get_parent(path)\r\n+    fs_type = H.fs_get_type(path)\r\n+    if fs_type ~= \"directory\" then H.error('`path` is not a valid directory (\"' .. path .. '\")') end\r\n+  end\r\n-  if fs_type == nil then H.error('`path` is not a valid path (\"' .. path .. '\")') end\r\n```\r\n\r\n",
